    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** standing as the undisputed anchor of the lineup. His 85.93 score is fueled by an excellent matchup against V\u00e1squez, whose 4.92 xERA invites aggression. **Corbin Carroll** receives a massive 19% boost for the favorable pitching skill gap and an additional 10% for his prime leadoff placement, making him our highest-upside asset for stolen bases. Meanwhile, **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (69.15) provide significant value; **Mickey Moniak** specifically benefits from a potent 14% platoon edge against Peralta and a favorable park factor, positioning him as a high-efficiency play despite batting in the heart of the order.\n\nTactical adjustments remain critical, particularly regarding our middle-infield rotation. **Travis Bazzana** (40.27) barely clung to his starting spot, his score suppressed by a difficult L/L matchup against Manaea. He narrowly edged out **Zach McKinstry** (38.04), whose lack of high-leverage lineup placement and poor park factors pushed him beneath our 40.0 baseline for efficiency. While we are currently optimizing for roster spots, we continue to monitor the status of **James Wood** and **Jose Caballero**, who remain on the bench due to lineup absences, while **Trevor Story** remains sidelined on the IL. We are also tracking the progress of **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Max Muncy**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte** for potential spot-start value in upcoming series.\n\nRisk mitigation is central to today\u2019s deployment, particularly with **Seiya Suzuki** (51.26). While he remains a strong start, he faces a stern test against Skubal, whose 2.67 xERA acts as a significant drag on our ceiling; we are essentially banking on **Seiya Suzuki** to maintain his 8% lineup-spot premium despite the elite pitching threat. Similarly, **Cole Young** (44.62) faces a defensive bottleneck as Melton\u2019s ability to hold runners limits his base-stealing upside, dropping him near the bottom of our preferred starts. Monitor the wind and late-breaking weather reports, as any shift in conditions could necessitate a quick pivot for the bottom third of our order.",
